Stop GiNaC::power::real_part()/imag_part() from raising "pow(0,0) is undefined" on an integer power
whose basis has a zero real or a zero imaginary part.

Both build Re/Im of (a+I*b)^N from the binomial expansion and write each term as
binomial(NN,n) * pow(a, NN-n) * pow(b, n). A binomial expansion needs the 0^0 == 1 convention for
its end terms, and GiNaC's pow() refuses: pow(0,0) throws. So (I*x)^2, whose expansion contains the
factor a^0 with a == Re(I*x) == 0, aborts code generation with a ValueError that says nothing about
where it came from. Every terminating case is unchanged: pow(x,0) is 1 for every x for which the
old code did not throw.

pyoomph reaches it from the azimuthal real/imaginary split. subexpression() now reports the real and
imaginary parts of a marker whose content is provably real (that is what keeps the split from
multiplying products out, see dev_docs/subexpression_unit_analysis_stall.md), so mul::find_real_imag
can now conclude that the real part of a factor is exactly zero, where before it produced a held
real_part() node that was never zero. The GiNaC bug is independent of pyoomph - "ex e = pow(I*x, 2);
e.real_part();" reproduces it - and is what to send upstream.

--- a/ginac/power.cpp
+++ b/ginac/power.cpp
